ple  as that um trevor i mean  we we don't know whether we're coming or  going in our industry we are losing uh  business we are losing livelihoods um i  have and  also very very important point because  we're talking about hospitality industry  here which which employs you know to  2.5 3 million people directly and  what about the ecosystem around the  hospitality industry so  all the drivers that come and deliver  our goods all the security the cleaners  the  the collection of refuse the suppliers  the supply chain is immense i've i've  had supplies on the phone in tears  asking me to take some produce but i  can't take produce i've had a collapse  of bookings  and i don't know whether i'm going to be  open tomorrow let alone next week so you  know we need clear decisions and if we  get locked down um then  we need compensation we need financial  help and again i reiterate not just the  hospitality industry but across the  board  we need proper firm government and a  government that we can respect  give us a picture of what this level of  uncertainty means in your business for  you know one of your one of your  restaurants or one of your family's  restaurants  yes i mean i i tweeted recently last  week about my um daughter's restaurant  and this is a you know a new business a  new restaurant with with a big debt  and that's another thing a lot of  restaurants you know  small businesses  are already laden with debt and we're  getting help yeah we have to take a loan  out and that's another debt that's  adding debt on debt  so that i don't consider that help  financially but anyway that's another  story so  she she was um  building up to a prosperous december a  december that would see her through the  lean months of dry january uh and  february and march  they're  normally quiet months  so in this industry we need to build up  reserves in november and december to see  us through well that's gone that's  finished  nothing left so we're now she is now  into a lost situation business-wise uh  in december  the chancellor's just come back from  california industry leaders are saying  that some businesses are going to go out  go  going to sink they're going to go under  um  what what practical help  would work for you what would you like  to hear rishi sunak announce  once he's done his sums  um well first of all we need assurance  going forward so next year the vat is  likely to go back up to 20 well um  it would help immensely if it was either  kept at 12 and a half or taken back down  to five and business rates need sorting  out that it's the most unfair system  ever um and then  uh statutory sick pay is one that i  would like to see reformed as well  because at the moment um people who are  catching covid uh with very mild  symptoms um are thinking well shall i go  to work or shall i you know  shall i just take statutory sick pay you  can't survive on that you can't pay the  rent you can't  there's no way so and then the burden is  put on back on the employer that's not  right so you know  there are a number of number of things  but if we are going to go into lockdown  and as i said i'm not against lockdown  i'm really not if it's for the right  reason but we need to know  we can't have this dilly dallying around  and this waffle that's coming out of the  government at the moment  um  you are of course uh proudly british  british michelle rue we claim you 100 as  our own but of course you have some  french heritage um  what do you think about the french uh  decision to essentially ban travel  between here and there  uh what it's a firm decision  uh i mean the one thing whether you you  you know you like or dislike mac hall he  takes decisions uh and that's another  thing i think there's a lot of  politicking going on between mack hall  and uh  uh and boris johnson um  yeah well one thinking he's uh napoleon  bonaparte and the other one thinking  he's winston churchill uh come on guys  get out get out of the playground and  and 